(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_surfacechemistry=[{id:"neet-2025-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Catalysts in industrial processes",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Haber process \u2014 I. Fe catalyst
B. Wacker oxidation \u2014 II. PdCl\u2082
C. Wilkinson catalyst \u2014 III. [(PPh\u2083)\u2083RhCl]
D. Ziegler catalyst \u2014 IV. TiCl\u2084 with Al(CH\u2083)\u2083
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],c:2,s:`A. Haber process uses Fe catalyst: A-I.
B. Wacker oxidation uses PdCl\u2082: B-II.
C. Wilkinson catalyst is [(PPh\u2083)\u2083RhCl]: C-III.
D. Ziegler catalyst is TiCl\u2084 with Al(CH\u2083)\u2083: D-IV.`},{id:"neet-2023-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis",q:"Which one is an example of heterogenous catalysis ?",o:["Combination between dinitrogen and dihydrogen to form ammonia in the presence of finely divided iron","Oxidation of sulphur dioxide into sulphur trioxide in the presence of oxides of nitrogen","Hydrolysis of sugar catalysed by H\u207A ions","Decomposition of ozone in presence of nitrogen monoxide"],c:0,s:`In heterogeneous catalysis the catalyst is in a different phase from the reactants.
N\u2082(g) + 3H\u2082(g) \u2192 2NH\u2083(g) with solid Fe: catalyst is solid, reactants are gases, so it is heterogeneous.
The other three are homogeneous: sugar hydrolysis with H\u2082SO\u2084 (liquid), 2SO\u2082 + O\u2082 with NO (gas), and ozone with NO (gas).`},{id:"neet-2023-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Types of colloids",q:"Pumice stone is an example of",o:["Foam","Sol","Gel","Solid sol"],c:3,s:`Pumice stone is a solid sol.
The dispersed phase is gas and the dispersion medium is solid.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q066",pn:66,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Adsorption isotherms",q:`Shown below are adsorption isotherms for a gas 'X' at temperatures T\u2081, T\u2082 and T\u2083 :
p and x/m represent pressure and extent of adsorption, respectively. The correct order of temperatures for the given, adsorption is :`,o:["T\u2081 = T\u2082 > T\u2083","T\u2081 > T\u2082 > T\u2083","T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081","T\u2081 = T\u2082 = T\u2083"],c:2,s:`As temperature rises, adsorption of a gas on a solid falls.
The curve for T\u2081 is highest and T\u2083 is lowest, so T\u2081 has the most adsorption.
Hence T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081.`,fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Adsorption isotherms: x/m against P for T1, T2 and T3, three curves rising from the origin and levelling off, T1 highest and T3 lowest.",d:[["l",50,140,50,14,"a"],["l",50,140,232,140,"a"],["t",44,70,"x/m","e"],["t",140,162,"P","m"],["p","M50 140C70 78 108 44 188 32",""],["p","M50 140C72 92 108 64 188 55",""],["p","M50 140C74 106 110 84 188 78",""],["t",194,36,"T\u2081",""],["t",194,59,"T\u2082",""],["t",194,82,"T\u2083",""]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Hardy-Schulze rule",q:`Given below are two statements
Statement I: In the coagulation of a negative sol, the flocculating power of the three given ions is in the order Al\xB3\u207A > Ba\xB2\u207A > Na\u207A
Statement II: In the coagulation of a positive sol, the flocculating power of the three given salts is in the order NaCl > Na\u2082SO\u2084 > Na\u2083PO\u2084
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct."],c:1,s:`By the Hardy-Schulze rule, flocculating power grows with the charge of the ion that has the opposite charge to the sol.
For a negative sol the cations act: Al\xB3\u207A > Ba\xB2\u207A > Na\u207A, so Statement I is correct.
For a positive sol the anions act: NaCl < Na\u2082SO\u2084 < Na\u2083PO\u2084.
Statement II gives the reverse order, so it is wrong.`},{id:"neet-2021-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Tyndall effect",q:'The right option for the statement "Tyndall effect is exhibited by", is :',o:["Urea solution","NaCl solution","Glucose solution","Starch solution"],c:3,s:`Tyndall effect (scattering of light by particles) happens only in colloids.
Urea, NaCl and glucose solutions are true solutions with tiny particles.
Starch solution is a colloid, so it shows the effect.`},{id:"neet-2020-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Colloids",q:"Measuring Zeta potential is useful in determining which property of colloidal solution ?",o:["Solubility","Stability of the colloidal particles","Size of the colloidal particles","Viscosity"],c:1,s:`Zeta potential is the potential difference across the fixed and diffuse layers of the charge on a colloid particle.
A larger zeta potential means more repulsion between particles, so the sol is more stable.`},{id:"neet-2019-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Colloids",q:"Which mixture of the solution will lead to the formation of negatively charged colloidal [AgI]I\u207B",o:["50 mL of 2 M AgNO\u2083 + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI","50 mL of 0.1 M Ag NO\u2083 + 50 mL of 0.1 M KI","50 mL of 1 M AgNO\u2083 + 50 mL of 1.5 M KI","50 mL of M Ag NO\u2083 + 50 mL of 2 M KI"],a:"3,4",s:`AgNO\u2083 + KI \u2192 AgI + KNO\u2083.
AgI adsorbs I\u207B ions (to give [AgI]I\u207B) only when KI is in excess.
Option 1: 100 mmol Ag\u207A against 75 mmol I\u207B, so Ag\u207A is in excess (positive sol).
Option 2: equal amounts, no excess.
Option 3: 50 mmol Ag\u207A against 75 mmol I\u207B, so KI is in excess.
Option 4: KI is 100 mmol and AgNO\u2083 is below that, so KI is in excess.
So options 3 and 4 are correct.
Note: Printed key is two options, 3 and 4. The AgNO\u2083 strength in option 4 is printed as 'M' with the number missing.`},{id:"neet-2018-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Hardy\u2013Schulze rule",q:"On which of the following properties does the coagulating power of an ion depend ?",o:["The magnitude of the charge on the ion alone","Size of the ion alone","Both magnitude and sign of the charge on the ion","The sign of charge on the ion alone"],c:2,s:`By the Hardy\u2013Schulze rule, the ion carrying a charge opposite to that of the colloid does the coagulating.
Its power grows with the magnitude of that charge.
So it depends on both the sign and the magnitude of the charge.`}]})();
